                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "Thought: We need to find all divisors of n greater than 1, then arrange them in a circle such that no two adjacent divisors are relatively prime. This requires us to factorize n, generate its divisors, filter out those greater than 1, and then find a circular arrangement that meets the condition. The run_sympy tool is suitable for factorization and generating divisors, while arranging them will require custom logic.\n\nAction: run_sympy\n```python\nfrom sympy import divisors, gcd\n\n# Compute n\nn = 90 * 140 * 13 * 19\n\n# Generate all divisors of n\ndivisors_n = divisors(n)\n\n# Filter out divisors greater than 1\nfiltered_divisors = [d for d in divisors_n if d > 1]\n\n# Function to find a valid circular arrangement of divisors\ndef find_arrangement(divisors):\n    # Start with the smallest divisor\n    arrangement = [divisors.pop(0)]\n    \n    # Try to append the next divisor that has a common factor with the last added\n    while divisors:\n        for i, d in enumerate(divisors):\n            if gcd(arrangement[-1], d) > 1:\n                arrangement.append(d)\n                del divisors[i]\n                break\n        else:\n            return None  # No valid arrangement found\n    \n    # Check if the first and last elements also share a common factor\n    if gcd(arrangement[0], arrangement[-1]) > 1:\n        return arrangement\n    else:\n        return None\n\n# Find a valid arrangement\nvalid_arrangement = find_arrangement(filtered_divisors.copy())\n\n# If we found a valid arrangement, map it back to the original indices\nif valid_arrangement:\n    original_indices = [filtered_divisors.index(d) + 1 for d in valid_arrangement]\nelse:\n    original_indices = []\n\nprint(original_indices)\n```\n```"
